 source the actual source is  the fourth link in the article the first  three links all take you to more  indieWIRE content more than once I've  accidentally clicked on one of these  links thinking it will take me to the  source only to find myself looking at  another article by the same publication  so at this point as the reader all  is to read the original interview of the  statement was in but I've already given  two page views to a publication for  content that isn't theirs maybe you  don't share my frustration maybe you  don't have time to read a long Vanity  Fair profile on Amelia Clark but you're  glad somebody else is reading it and  putting the most interesting pieces into  an article that you can read in two  minutes  I dislike these articles but I don't  want to strawman my opponent so let me  make the best case I can for why  headline mining might be a good thing I  didn't even bother reading this box  article that was basically a review of  The Rolling Stones recent Johnny Depp  profile but I did find out about and  read The Rolling Stones article because  of it in a way the headline mining  article can almost act as an ad or  promotion for the larger article so  these smaller articles might get the  attention of people who never would have  read the full piece smart news  publications will do this with their own  interviews if something significant is  set sometimes a quote will itself become  a news event and it makes sense for  other news outlets to report on it  ideally I think these reports should add  commentary context or discussion to the  report rather than just simply  summarizing the contents of the  interview or adding a goofy quip here's  an example of this type of article done  well this is from birth movies death  calm and it's essentially just reporting  on something the site Collider reported  in an article but they are upfront in  the headline about what they're doing  they add context that's valuable  then they clearly encourage the 